%I A395821 #39 Sep 23 2026 23:38:37
%S A395821 1,3,5,7,9,11,14,15,18,19
%N A395821 Maximum number of points that can be chosen from an n X n grid so that no four of them lie on a common circle or on a common line.
%C A395821 This is the no-four-on-a-circle problem of Erdős and Purdy (Guy, Unsolved Problems in Number Theory, Problem F3), in the form studied by Thiele, where lines count as degenerate circles.
%C A395821 Thiele proved a(n) <= floor((5n-3)/2). Ghosal et al. (2026) proved a(n) >= 2n - o(n) and gave lower bounds for n <= 30 (their Appendix B): 1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11, 14, 15, 18, 19, 21, 23, 26, 27, 29, 31, 33, 35, 36, 38, 41, 42, 44, 45, 47, 49, 50, 52, 54, 55. These are attained for n <= 10.
%C A395821 Terms for n = 2..10 were computed by exhaustive SAT search (with symmetry breaking and cube-and-conquer for n = 9 and 10). a(11) >= 21.
%C A395821 a(n) <= A399894(n), since every isosceles trapezium is a cyclic quadrilateral, and a(n) <= A072567(n), since an axis-parallel rectangle is concyclic.
%C A395821 The variant in which only circles of finite radius are forbidden (collinear quadruples allowed) gives a different sequence: it agrees with a(n) for n <= 7 and n = 9 but has 16 at n = 8.
%H A395821 A. Ghosal, R. Goenka, A. Grebennikov, P. Keevash, M. Kwan, and H. T. Pham, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.05255">No-(k+1)-in-line problem for k >= 3</a>, arXiv:2607.05255 [math.CO], 2026. See Appendix B.
%H A395821 Azmi C. Özgen, <a href="/A395821/a395821.txt">Python program</a>
%H A395821 T. Thiele, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/0097-3165(95)90007-1">The no-four-on-circle problem</a>, J. Combin. Theory Ser. A 71 (1995), 332-334.
%e A395821 For n = 3 the five points (0,2), (1,1), (1,2), (1,0), (2,0) have no four on a circle or line, and no six points of the 3 X 3 grid do (any six of nine cells contain either four on a line or four concyclic).
%Y A395821 Cf. A072567, A227133, A271906, A271907, A272651, A399894.
%K A395821 nonn,hard,more,new
%O A395821 1,2
